#!/bin/bash
# arc-gate-bypass — RETIRED. There is no bypass.
#
# The SOP gate has no override, no timer, and no owner backdoor: the only way
# to unlock operations is reading and accepting the current SOT + all MOs
# (arc-sop start "task"). The old version of this script wrote
# ~/.config/arc/gate-bypass and printed "Gate bypass ACTIVE" — but the gate
# stopped reading that file when overrides were retired, so the fire exit
# claimed success while nothing unlocked. That trap is gone. This script now
# tells the truth and points at recovery, and stays dependency-free on purpose
# so it works even when arc-sop itself is broken.
cat >&2 <<'EOF'
arc-gate-bypass is retired — there is no bypass.

The SOP gate has no override, no timer, and no owner backdoor. If you are
locked out, the recovery paths are:

  arc-sop emergency    read-only hatch: prints policy paths + recovery steps
  arc-sop recover      restore policy files (invalidates prior acceptances)
  arc-sop gate-status  show what the gate still needs

Operations unlock only by reading and accepting the current policy:

  arc-sop start "short task"
EOF
exit 1
